      ],
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 313699,
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 303460,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "text": "Memory Cell Transistor Circuit Topologies",
      "fontSize": 17,
      "fontFamily": 1,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "sub-mu42zdxy-30"
    },
    {
      "id": "sub-mu42zdxy-28-note",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -1193,
      "y": 1024,
      "width": 314,
      "height": 265,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "grp_mu42zdy2_2"
      ],
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 693848,
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 227193,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "text": "ROM retains data permanently without\nelectrical power. Masked ROM is\nfactory-programmed via photolithography\nmasks for mass firmware. PROM uses\nfusible links burned once by\nhigh-current pulses. EPROM uses\nfloating-gate charge traps erased by\nexposing its quartz window to UV light\n(253.7 nm) for 20 minutes. EEPROM\nsupports byte-level electrical erasure\nvia Fowler-Nordheim tunneling. Flash\nMemory wipes data in large 4 KB to 64 KB\nblocks for high throughput in SSDs and\nBIOS chips.",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 1,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "edge-sub-mu42zdxy-28-sub-mu42zdxy-28-note",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "sub-mu42zdxy-30-note",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -1287,
      "y": 1330,
      "width": 306,
      "height": 114,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "grp_mu42zdy2_2"
      ],
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 173922,
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 281114,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "text": "The physical silicon topologies define\nspeed and density trade-offs: SRAM uses\ncross-coupled inverters with word line\ngates, while DRAM uses a single\npass-transistor gating storage charge\ninto a microscopic cell capacitor.",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 1,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "edge-sub-mu42zdxy-30-sub-mu42zdxy-30-note",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
